---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import ChuckDiagram from '../components/ChuckDiagram.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import bottlingLine from '../assets/images/bottling-line.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Capping Chucks: Types, Torque Specs, and How They Work"
    description="What a capping chuck is, how it grips and torques a bottle cap, the main chuck types, and the torque, speed, and size numbers that matter. Plain-English guide to bottle capping chucks."
    ogTitle="Capping Chucks: Types, Torque Specs, and How They Work"
    ogDescription="Plain-English guide to bottle capping chucks: how they work, chuck types, torque specs, and calibration."
>
    <PageHero
        title="Capping Chucks"
        answerLabel="What is a capping chuck?"
        image={bottlingLine}
        alt="Wine bottles moving through a rotary capping station on an industrial bottling line"
        caption="Rotary capping station on a bottling line. Photo: Pexels"
    >
        <p>A capping chuck is the part of a capping machine that grabs a bottle cap, spins it onto the bottle, and stops at a set <a href="/glossary/#torque">torque</a> (twisting force). One chuck sits at the bottom of each spinning spindle.</p>
    </PageHero>

    <main>
        <section id="fast-facts" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Capping chucks by the numbers</h2>
                <div class="facts">
                    <div class="fact"><span class="fact-num">0.3&ndash;8 N&middot;m</span><span class="fact-label">Typical application torque, from 8 mm to 120 mm caps (<a href="/cap-torque/#chart">chart</a>)</span></div>
                    <div class="fact"><span class="fact-num">40&ndash;60%</span><span class="fact-label">Removal torque after 24 hours, as a share of application torque</span></div>
                    <div class="fact"><span class="fact-num">&le; 5%</span><span class="fact-label">A common target for torque spread (<a href="/glossary/#cv">CV%</a>)</span></div>
                    <div class="fact"><span class="fact-num">1,400</span><span class="fact-label">Caps per minute on the fastest single rotary cappers (84,000 per hour)</span></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="how-it-works" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>What a capping chuck does, step by step</h2>
                <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 items-start">
                    <ol class="steps">
                        <li>The chuck comes down over a loose cap sitting on the bottle.</li>
                        <li>A rubber or plastic <a href="/glossary/#insert">insert</a> inside the chuck grips the ridges (<a href="/glossary/#knurling">knurling</a>) on the side of the cap.</li>
                        <li>The spindle spins the chuck. The cap threads onto the bottle neck.</li>
                        <li>When the cap hits the set <a href="/cap-torque/">torque</a>, the clutch above the chuck slips. The cap stops turning, so it is never too tight.</li>
                        <li>The chuck lets go and lifts. The next bottle moves in.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <ChuckDiagram />
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="grip-styles" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>How bottle capping chucks grip the cap</h2>
                <p class="lead prose-col">Chucks differ in two ways: how they hold the cap, and what controls the torque. Here are the four common ways to hold the cap. For torque control, see <a href="/chuck-types/">capping chuck types</a>.</p>
                <div class="cards cards-2">
                    <div class="card card-blue">
                        <h3>One-piece insert chuck</h3>
                        <p>A solid metal cup, usually aluminum or stainless steel. A rubber or urethane liner sits inside. The liner is shaped to fit one cap size. Simple, cheap, and the most common style.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-red">
                        <h3>Jaw chuck</h3>
                        <p>Three or four metal or plastic jaws close around the cap. Jaws can handle caps with odd shapes and features that a plain cup cannot grip.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-yellow">
                        <h3>Pneumatic chuck</h3>
                        <p>Air pressure closes jaws or a flexible collar around the cap. Change the air pressure to change the grip. Good for lines that switch between many cap types.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-green">
                        <h3>Vacuum chuck</h3>
                        <p>Suction pulls the cap up into the chuck and holds it centered. Used for thin or light caps that squeezing would bend. <a href="/chuck-types/#vacuum">More on vacuum cap chucks</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="chuck-vs-headset" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>The chuck is one part of the headset</h2>
                <p>The chuck only grips the cap. The full unit on the spindle is called a capping headset, or capping head. The headset holds the chuck plus the clutch that controls torque.</p>
                <p>On fast rotary lines, that clutch is usually magnetic. To learn how those work, read our guide to <a href="/capping-headsets/">magnetic capping headsets</a>. Chucks are used on <a href="/capping-machines/#chuck-capper">chuck cappers</a>. Spindle cappers and crown cappers don't use them.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="guides" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Pick a guide</h2>
                <div class="cards cards-2">
                    <a href="/chuck-types/" class="card card-link card-blue">
                        <h3>Capping Chuck Types</h3>
                        <p>Magnetic, friction, pneumatic, and vacuum chucks. How each one works and when to use it.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/capping-headsets/" class="card card-link card-red">
                        <h3>Magnetic Capping Headsets</h3>
                        <p>How magnetic headsets set torque with no parts rubbing, plus spring-clutch, pneumatic, servo, and ROPP heads.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/capping-machines/" class="card card-link card-green">
                        <h3>Capping Machine Types</h3>
                        <p>Chuck, spindle, snap, ROPP, crown, and lug cappers. How each one applies a cap and which ones use chucks.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/cap-torque/" class="card card-link card-yellow">
                        <h3>Cap Torque Guide</h3>
                        <p>Application vs. removal torque, a torque chart by cap size, unit conversions, and torque testers.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/cap-sizes/" class="card card-link card-blue">
                        <h3>Cap and Neck Finish Sizes</h3>
                        <p>What codes like 38-400 and PCO 1881 mean, and how to size a chuck to your cap.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/ropp-capping/" class="card card-link card-red">
                        <h3>ROPP Capping</h3>
                        <p>How roll-on pilfer-proof aluminum caps are formed on the bottle, head setup, and common defects.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/troubleshooting/" class="card card-link card-yellow">
                        <h3>Troubleshooting and Calibration</h3>
                        <p>Fix scuffed caps, uneven torque, broken tamper bands, and cross-threading. Step-by-step calibration.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/oem-compatibility/" class="card card-link card-green">
                        <h3>OEM Compatibility</h3>
                        <p>Zalkin, Krones, AROL, CSI, and Tedelta cappers, and what to check before buying parts.</p>
                    </a>
                    <a href="/glossary/" class="card card-link card-green">
                        <h3>Glossary</h3>
                        <p>Plain definitions for torque, knurling, top load, slip torque, 29/25, PCO 1881, and more.</p>
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <FAQ />
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
